task: stream-event-time-window
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/stream-event-time-window
worktree: ../okay-wt-event-window
spec: specs/event-time-windows.md (new)

why: the operator's call after flink-stream-bench (2026-09-10) — "делай
  всё что ты обнаружил". §20 of docs/benchmarks.md measured the gap and
  named it: Flink's stage 2 is `.window(TumblingEventTimeWindows.of(…))`,
  one line; the benchmark's own `Windows` is fifty, and every user of
  okay who windows an event-time stream writes them again. The core has
  the arithmetic (`Aggregator`) and the pass (`Chunks`) and nothing in
  between. specs/aggregators.md's sliding window is by ELEMENT COUNT on
  a Group; this is by event TIME on any Aggregator, which is a different
  operator.

what: `Windows` in the core (cross-platform, no Async): keyed panes for
  tumbling and sliding event-time windows over an `Aggregator`, a
  bounded-out-of-orderness watermark, eviction as the watermark passes
  a window's end, and late events DROPPED and counted rather than
  silently folded into a closed window. Plus the composable form — a
  `Stage[A, Pane[K, O], Unit]` — so a window sits in a pipeline
  (`through`) rather than only in a hand-written loop. Tests: equality
  with a reference recompute-from-scratch fold on generated data,
  sliding panes = size/slide, late drops counted, and the same value
  driven twice giving the same answer (a Stage is a VALUE).

predict: the class form costs what the benchmark's hand-written one
  costs (it IS that code, generalised over the key); the Stage form
  pays the Take/Writer coroutine per element and will be measurably
  slower — both go in §20 rather than only the flattering one.
